<Base
  title="The limits of honor"
  description="Iliad Book 22: Hector faces Achilles, Athena deceives him, and Achilles drags the body. Honor without a limit becomes savagery."
>
  <LessonChrome slug="the-limits-of-honor">
    <header class="page-head">
      <h1>The limits of honor</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Today we add a limit that honor, by itself, does not know. Hector can
        face certain death. Achilles cannot tell himself when to stop.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="learn" aria-labelledby="will-learn">
      <h2 id="will-learn">What you’ll learn</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Hector stays outside the walls though Priam and Hecuba beg him to come in. He chooses to face Achilles.</li>
        <li>Athena, in the shape of Deiphobus, persuades him to stand. The help he thinks he has is a god’s trick.</li>
        <li>He asks that the winner not outrage the corpse. Achilles refuses any ransom and says dogs and vultures will eat the body.</li>
        <li>He pierces the heels, ties the body to the chariot, and drags it. Honor without restraint has become savagery. The gods’ objection is the next book.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <div class="lesson-body">
      <p class="scene-today">Today we add a limit honor does not know to the foundation.</p>
      <h3>The man who stays outside</h3>
      <p>
        Let’s begin at the walls. The Trojans are inside, drinking, leaning on the
        battlements. Hector stays before the Scaean gates. Priam sees Achilles
        coming across the plain, bright as the star called Orion’s Hound, which
        bodes fever and fire. The old man reaches out his arms and begs his son to
        come inside. Hecuba bares the breast that nursed him and asks the same.
        If Achilles kills him outside, she says, the dogs at the ships will devour
        him, and she and his wife will never weep over his bed. Hector does not
        move. His heart is set on the fight.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is courage in front of death he can already see. It is the same
        <em>aidōs</em> as at the gate in Book 6, now with the champion actually in
        sight. The poem does not call the choice wise. It shows you a man who will
        not come in, and two parents who know what the dogs will do.
      </p>

      <h3>A brother who is not there</h3>
      <p>
        Achilles chases him round the city. They pass the springs of Scamander and
        the washing-troughs where, in peacetime, the Trojan women washed clothes.
        The prize is Hector’s life. Athena leaves Achilles and comes to Hector as
        Deiphobus, the brother he trusts. Hector is grateful that this brother
        came outside when the others stayed in. Athena’s trick is the reason he
        stops running. He says he will no longer fly. He asks for pledges: the
        winner may strip the armour, but he is to give the body back and not treat
        it in any unseemly fashion. Achilles answers, “Fool, prate not to me about
        covenants. There can be no covenants between men and lions.” He kills him
        at the throat, the one place the taken armour does not cover.
      </p>
      <p>
        Dying, Hector asks by Achilles’ life, his knees, and his parents that the
        dogs not devour him at the ships, and that the body go home for the fire.
        Achilles answers, “Dog.” He wishes he could cut the flesh and eat it raw.
        Nothing will save Hector from the dogs, not ten or twenty times the
        ransom, not his weight in gold from Priam. Dogs and vultures will eat him
        utterly up. Hector’s soul goes down lamenting. The other Greeks come up
        and each gives the body a fresh wound.
      </p>

      <h3>What honor does when nothing stops it</h3>
      <p>
        Then Achilles treats the body with contumely. He pierces the sinews at the
        back of both feet, passes thongs of ox-hide through, and makes the body
        fast to the chariot, the head trailing. He lashes the horses. The dust
        rises from Hector. The dark hair flies. The head that was comely is laid
        in the dust. The poem says Zeus had delivered him to his foes to do him
        outrage in his own land. Hecuba tears her hair. Priam groans. The people
        wail as if the city were on fire. Priam wants to go alone to the ships and
        ask for compassion. The book does not yet give it to him.
      </p>
      <p>
        Look back at the foundation. Lesson 2: honor is a prize other men can see,
        and wrath when it is taken. Lesson 4: honor can be duty to a house. Lesson 5:
        loyalty brought Achilles back into the war. None of those steps tells him
        to stop once Hector is dead. The code that made him fight has no sentence
        in it for the corpse. That is the failure this lesson exists to show.
        Honor alone does not tell a man when to stop. The gods’ displeasure, and
        the pity that answers it, are Book 24. Do not borrow them yet. Sit with
        the dragging.
      </p>
      <p>
        A profession has its own version of the unstopped fight: the case you
        keep punishing after it is won, the colleague you keep diminishing after
        the point is made. The poem’s picture is worse, and it is exact. Courage
        got Hector to the meeting. It did not give Achilles a limit. The next
        lesson is the limit, and it comes from grief shared with an enemy, not
        from more honor of the same kind.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Reader
      heading="The text: Iliad, Book 22"
      note="Samuel Butler, 1898, in full, from Wikisource, The Iliad (Butler), Book XXII. Achaeans is spelled without the æ of the earlier books. Misprints on that page are left as printed."
      sourceHref={book22.source}
      paragraphs={book22.paragraphs}
      glossary={[
        { word: "Scaean", note: "The gates of Troy where Hector waits." },
        { word: "Deiphobus", note: "Hector’s brother. Athena takes his shape." },
        { word: "Minerva", note: "Butler’s name for Athena." },
        { word: "Jove", note: "Butler’s name for Zeus." },
      ]}
    />

    <figure class="figure lesson-figure">
      <Image
        src={plate}
        alt="Line engraving of a warrior in a chariot dragging a naked body, with a second figure running behind."
        width={1600}
        height={1186}
      />
      <figcaption>
        A body dragged behind a chariot. Engraving by Tommaso Piroli, 1795, after
        John Flaxman’s drawing of 1793. Wikimedia Commons files this plate under
        “Achilles and the body of Hector.” Public domain. The photograph of the
        sheet is by H.-P. Haack.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="word-day" aria-labelledby="word-day">
      <h2 id="word-day">Word of the day</h2>
      <p class="word-greek">contumely</p>
      <p>
        Butler’s word for what Achilles does to the body: “he treated the body of
        Hector with contumely.” It means a deliberate outrage, done so that
        others can see it. Find the sentence in the reader, then the thongs
        through the heels. The limit this word marks is the one honor did not
        supply.
      </p>
    </section>

    <Reveal
      items={[
        {
          prompt: "Who begs Hector to come inside, and does he?",
          answer:
            "Priam and Hecuba. He stays before the gates. His heart is set on fighting Achilles.",
        },
        {
          prompt: "How does Athena get Hector to stop running?",
          answer:
            "She takes the shape and voice of Deiphobus and offers to stand with him. Hector thinks his brother has come outside for his sake.",
        },
        {
          prompt: "What bargain does Hector offer, and what does Achilles answer?",
          answer:
            "Hector asks that the winner give the body back and not outrage it. Achilles refuses. He says dogs and vultures will eat the body, though Priam offer Hector’s weight in gold.",
        },
        {
          prompt: "What does Achilles do with the body after the other Greeks have stabbed it?",
          answer:
            "He pierces the heels, ties the body to his chariot with ox-hide thongs, and drags it, the head in the dust.",
        },
      ]}
    />

    <section class="reflection" aria-labelledby="reflect-title">
      <h2 id="reflect-title">A short reflection</h2>
      <p>
        If you want one sentence: where have you watched a justified anger keep
        going after the person was already beaten? It stays in this browser.
      </p>
      <label class="reflection-label" for="note-limits">Your note</label>
      <textarea id="note-limits" data-note="the-limits-of-honor" rows="4"></textarea>
    </section>

    <section class="takeaways" aria-labelledby="takeaways-title">
      <h2 id="takeaways-title">Key takeaways</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>The earlier steps — public honor, duty to a house, loyalty to a friend — are all still in this death. None of them tells Achilles to stop.</li>
        <li>Hector’s courage is real, and it is aimed at a death he has been warned about by his father and his mother.</li>
        <li>Athena’s trick removes the brother’s help he thought he had. The stand is his. The deception is the god’s.</li>
        <li>The dragging is honor without a limit. The poem’s own word, in Butler, is contumely. Pity has not arrived.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="summary">
        Honor could get a man to fight and could not tell him when the fight was
        over. The body in the dust is the proof. The next lesson is what happens
        when the father of that body comes to the tent.
      </p>
    </section>

    <aside class="go-deeper">
      <h2>Go deeper, if you want</h2>
      <p>
        Book 19, the reconciliation with Agamemnon, is optional in the syllabus.
        The Lion Gate is the monument the syllabus names again this week. You do
        not need either to finish Book 22.
      </p>
    </aside>

